The bug in `fio_keyword_replace`:
```c
opt += strlen(kw->word) + olen;
if (strlen(opt))
    memcpy(new + olen + len, opt, opt - o_org - 1);
```

`opt - o_org - 1` = `strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1`. This is the length of the prefix+keyword minus 1, NOT the length of the remaining string. So `memcpy` reads `olen + strlen(kw->word) - 1` bytes from `opt` (which points to the remaining string). If the remaining string is shorter than that, it reads past the end of the buffer → heap-buffer-overflow.

The fuzz target takes the input as a job file (INI format). The last byte is the type for `parse_jobs_ini`. Let me check what type value is needed. Let me look at `parse_jobs_ini`.

